-- allium: 3
-- sse_wire.allium
--
-- SSE Wire Protocol: Server-Side Contract
--
-- Scope: The SSE wire protocol — what the server is obliged to emit,
--        in what order, and under what conditions. The server-side
--        dual to conversation_atom.allium (the client-side reducer).
--
-- Includes: All nine SSE event types (init, message, message_updated,
--           state_change, token, agent_done, conversation_became_terminal,
--           conversation_update, error); ordering constraints between them;
--           the persist-before-broadcast invariant; broadcast-lag semantics;
--           the per-conversation ReplayRing that buffers ephemeral events
--           between persisted Message anchors so reconnecting clients can
--           resume mid-stream views (in-flight assistant message + state
--           changes + streaming tokens). The distinction between persisted
--           and ephemeral Message broadcasts (send_persisted_message vs
--           send_ephemeral_message) lives at the broadcaster API only —
--           the wire variant is shared.
--
-- Excludes: Client-side reducer behaviour (see conversation_atom.allium).
--           Client reconnect lifecycle (see connection_machine.allium, task 02681).
--           Bedrock state machine internals (see bedrock.allium).
--           HTTP transport mechanics, TLS, authentication.
--           Message content shape (opaque to this spec).
--
-- Self-contained: no `use` imports. External types (ConvState,
--           MessageContent, Conversation shape) are declared opaque.
--           Server-side specs govern their internals.
--
-- Rationale: task 02679 (streaming-finalizes-then-disappears) identified
--           a class of bugs at the ordering boundary between DB persistence
--           and SSE broadcast. Naming the ordering invariants formally makes
--           emit-vs-persist races a spec violation rather than an open mystery.
--
-- Cross-references:
--   specs/conversation_atom/conversation_atom.allium — client reducer (the dual)
--   tasks/02679 — streaming-finalize-disappear bug; PersistBeforeBroadcast is
--                 the formal statement of the leading fix hypothesis
--   tasks/02681 — connection_machine.allium (reconnect lifecycle)
--   src/api/wire.rs — SseWireEvent, the Rust wire type
--   src/runtime.rs  — SseBroadcaster, sequence counter, send_message/send_seq
--   src/api/sse.rs  — sse_stream, BroadcastStreamRecvError::Lagged handling

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Data carried in the init event. The reducer (conversation_atom.allium)
-- governs how each field is applied. This spec stores last_sequence_id
-- on the stream to anchor the sequence ordering guarantee.
--
-- `pending_events` are the contents of the per-conversation ReplayRing
-- at subscribe time: every event broadcast since the last persisted
-- Message that has not yet been absorbed into a durable DB row. The
-- reducer replays them through its normal per-event rules after the
-- DB snapshot lands so a reconnect during an in-flight turn (mid-LLM
-- stream, mid-tool-execution) does not blank out the UI's in-progress
-- state.
--
-- `pending_anchor_sequence_id` is the sequence_id of the last persisted
-- Message at subscribe time — i.e., the seq corresponding to the most
-- recent durable fact already in `messages`. Every entry in
-- `pending_events` has sequence_id > pending_anchor_sequence_id; the
-- client uses this as the floor for `applyIfNewer` when replaying.
--
-- `pending_truncated` is true iff the ring overflowed (capacity reached
-- and oldest entries evicted) between the anchor and the current tip.
-- A truncated replay produces a partial in-flight view; the client can
-- surface this to the user analogously to the broadcast-lag close path.
value InitSnapshot {
    last_sequence_id: Integer
    pending_anchor_sequence_id: Integer
    pending_events: List<PendingEventEntry>
    pending_truncated: Boolean
    -- conversation, messages, breadcrumbs, agent_working, display_state,
    -- context_window_size, model_context_window, project_name: opaque at this level.
}

-- One entry in the per-conversation replay ring. Each carries the
-- original sequence_id assigned by SseBroadcaster.next_seq when the
-- event was first emitted — that is, the entry's seq is the one the
-- client's reducer rules will see if/when the entry replays via init.
--
-- `event_type` matches the SseWireEvent's `type` discriminator
-- ("token", "state_change", "message", "message_updated",
-- "agent_done", "conversation_update", "conversation_became_terminal",
-- "error", "browser_session_state", "steer_message_queued"). Init
-- itself is never in the ring (init is per-subscriber, never
-- broadcast). ConversationHardDeleted is excluded by construction (a
-- deleted conversation cannot subsequently receive a subscribe).
--
-- `payload` is opaque at this level — it is whatever the wire-format
-- carries for that event_type. The replay path serialises the entry
-- back through SseWireEvent so the client cannot tell a replayed
-- event from a live one.
value PendingEventEntry {
    event_type: String
    sequence_id: Integer
    -- payload: opaque
}

-- A user-facing error broadcast as an error event. Carries a flat
-- message string (for toasts) and an opaque typed kind (for
-- future kind-aware affordances).
value UserFacingError {
    message: String
    -- error kind and details: opaque
}

-- A partial conversation metadata update broadcast as conversation_update.
-- All fields are optional; the client merges shallowly onto its local
-- Conversation copy.
value ConversationMetadataUpdate {
    cwd: String?
    -- branch_name, worktree_path, conv_mode_label, base_branch,
    -- task_title: opaque
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A message committed to SQLite for this conversation.
-- Created by MessageCommittedToDb when the DB write completes.
-- The sequence_id is pre-allocated by the broadcast channel before
-- the DB write, then observed back via SseBroadcaster.observe_seq().
entity PersistedMessage {
    conversation_id: String
    message_id: String
    sequence_id: Integer
}

-- Join entity: records that a message-type SSE event was delivered
-- on a specific stream. One StreamMessage is created per open stream
-- when MessageBroadcast fires. Used by PersistBeforeBroadcast to
-- assert every delivered message has a backing PersistedMessage.
entity StreamMessage {
    stream: SseStream
    message_id: String
    sequence_id: Integer
}

-- Per-conversation ring buffer of broadcast events between persisted
-- Messages. Anchored at the sequence_id of the most recent persisted
-- Message; entries are events with strictly greater seqs that have not
-- yet been collapsed into a durable DB row.
--
-- Created lazily on the conversation's first broadcast (or on the
-- first persisted Message, whichever comes first). One ring per
-- conversation; cleared on every persisted Message broadcast
-- (PersistedMessageClearsReplayRing rule) so the buffer never grows
-- without bound across multi-turn histories. Bounded by
-- `replay_ring_capacity`; oldest-first eviction on overflow sets
-- `truncated` until the next anchor reset.
--
-- The ring is NOT a substitute for the DB: durable state lives in
-- SQLite. The ring is a recovery aid for the narrow window between
-- one PersistedMessage and the next, where the UI's in-flight view
-- (streaming tokens, current_tool phase, eager assistant message
-- before its tool round completes) would otherwise vanish on reconnect.
entity ReplayRing {
    conversation_id: String
    -- The sequence_id of the persisted Message that last reset this
    -- ring. All entries have sequence_id strictly greater than this.
    -- Starts at 0 for a freshly-created conversation.
    anchor_sequence_id: Integer
    -- Whether the ring overflowed since the last anchor reset. Surfaced
    -- in InitSnapshot.pending_truncated; cleared on every anchor reset
    -- (every PersistedMessage broadcast).
    truncated: Boolean
    -- The retained entries, in broadcast order. length(entries) is
    -- bounded by config.replay_ring_capacity.
    entries: List<PendingEventEntry>
}

-- An active or closed SSE connection for one conversation.
-- Created when a client subscribes (GET /api/conversations/:id/events).
-- Closed when the client disconnects or broadcast lag is detected.
entity SseStream {
    conversation_id: String
    status: StreamStatus
    -- True once the init event has been delivered. No other event
    -- may be delivered before init_sent becomes true.
    init_sent: Boolean
    -- The sequence_id of the last event delivered on this stream.
    -- Starts at 0. Advances with every delivered event.
    last_delivered_seq: Integer
    -- The snapshot used to initialise this stream. Carries
    -- last_sequence_id (the DB watermark at subscribe time) and
    -- anchors the InitSnapshot type.
    init_snapshot: InitSnapshot
    -- The most recent user-facing error broadcast on this stream,
    -- or null if no error has been broadcast.
    last_error: UserFacingError?
    -- The most recent metadata update broadcast on this stream,
    -- or null if no update has been broadcast.
    last_update: ConversationMetadataUpdate?

    -- All message events delivered on this stream.
    stream_messages: StreamMessage with stream = this

    transitions status {
        open -> closed
        terminal: closed
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

enum StreamStatus { open | closed }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- Capacity of the per-conversation tokio broadcast channel.
    -- Sized for ~80 seconds at 50 tokens/second. Exceeding this
    -- triggers LagCloseStream.
    broadcast_capacity: Integer = 4096

    -- Capacity of the per-conversation ReplayRing. Bounds how many
    -- ephemeral events between two persisted Messages can be replayed
    -- on reconnect. At 50 tokens/sec, 512 entries covers ~10 seconds
    -- of LLM streaming — a typical mid-turn outage window. Overflow
    -- evicts oldest-first and sets the ring's truncated flag.
    replay_ring_capacity: Integer = 512
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules — stream lifecycle
------------------------------------------------------------

-- On subscribe, a new SseStream is created and the init event is
-- delivered as the very first event. init_sent is set true at
-- creation; no other rule may deliver events to a stream with
-- init_sent = false.
--
-- The init snapshot captures both the DB-durable state (messages,
-- conversation metadata, etc.) AND the current ReplayRing contents
-- so that a reconnect during an in-flight turn can resume the UI's
-- mid-stream view rather than blanking out until the next checkpoint.
rule StreamOpened {
    when: ClientSubscribes(conversation_id, snapshot)

    requires: snapshot.pending_anchor_sequence_id <= snapshot.last_sequence_id
    requires:
        for entry in snapshot.pending_events:
            entry.sequence_id > snapshot.pending_anchor_sequence_id
            and entry.sequence_id <= snapshot.last_sequence_id

    ensures: SseStream.created(
        conversation_id: conversation_id,
        status: open,
        init_sent: true,
        last_delivered_seq: snapshot.last_sequence_id,
        init_snapshot: snapshot
    )

    @guidance
        -- Implementation sequence is load-bearing: subscribe to the live
        -- broadcast receiver before reading DB messages and before
        -- snapshotting the ReplayRing. A persisted message that commits during
        -- stream open is then either present in the DB `messages` snapshot or
        -- delivered on the subscribed live receiver; init must not set
        -- `last_sequence_id`/`pending_anchor_sequence_id` past a message absent
        -- from both delivery paths. This is the concrete construction for
        -- InitSnapshotNoDurableGap.
        --
        -- The init payload is a consistent DB snapshot taken at subscribe
        -- time. It includes all persisted messages with sequence_id <=
        -- snapshot.pending_anchor_sequence_id. Because PersistBeforeBroadcast
        -- holds, any message that was already broadcast before this subscribe
        -- has sequence_id <= pending_anchor_sequence_id and appears in the
        -- snapshot — the client receives no gaps for durable state.
        --
        -- snapshot.pending_events captures the ReplayRing contents at
        -- subscribe time. Each entry's sequence_id is in
        -- (pending_anchor_sequence_id, last_sequence_id]. The client
        -- reducer replays them after applying the DB snapshot,
        -- re-anchoring streaming buffers / phase / in-flight assistant
        -- messages that would otherwise be lost. See
        -- conversation_atom.allium SseInitFreshConnect /
        -- SseInitReconnectMerge for the replay semantics.
        --
        -- init_sent is set true atomically with last_delivered_seq
        -- at creation, enforcing InitAlwaysFirst by construction:
        -- a stream that has observed any event (last_delivered_seq > 0)
        -- necessarily has init_sent = true.
}

-- When the broadcast channel reports a lag (the client fell so far
-- behind that the ring buffer wrapped), the stream closes. The client's
-- EventSource observes the close, its ConnectionMachine schedules a
-- reconnect, and the next subscribe delivers a fresh init that
-- resyncs from the DB.
rule LagCloseStream {
    when: BroadcastLagDetected(stream, lagged_by)

    requires: stream.status = open

    ensures: stream.status = closed

    @guidance
        -- Implementation: BroadcastStream::take_while returns false on
        -- BroadcastStreamRecvError::Lagged (src/api/sse.rs:sse_stream).
        -- A tracing::warn! is emitted with conversation_id and lagged_by.
        --
        -- No events are silently dropped: SQLite holds all state
        -- regardless of broadcast lag. The reconnect init snapshot is
        -- equivalent to a fresh load — no gaps visible to the client.
        -- See connection_machine.allium (task 02681) for the client-side
        -- reconnect lifecycle.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants — init snapshot gap freedom
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Init must never advance the client's sequence floor past durable or replayable
-- facts that the client did not receive. For persisted Messages at or below the
-- pending anchor, the DB `messages` snapshot is the delivery mechanism. For
-- events above the anchor and at or below the init tip, either durable state in
-- the DB snapshot subsumes them or the event appears in `pending_events`.
invariant InitSnapshotNoDurableGap {
    for stream in SseStreams:
        for msg in PersistedMessages:
            (msg.conversation_id = stream.conversation_id
             and msg.sequence_id <= stream.init_snapshot.pending_anchor_sequence_id) implies
                InitSnapshotIncludesMessage(stream.init_snapshot, msg)

    for stream in SseStreams:
        for entry in ReplayRing{conversation_id: stream.conversation_id}.entries:
            (entry.sequence_id > stream.init_snapshot.pending_anchor_sequence_id
             and entry.sequence_id <= stream.init_snapshot.last_sequence_id) implies
                InitSnapshotIncludesPendingEvent(stream.init_snapshot, entry)
                or InitSnapshotDurableStateSubsumesEvent(stream.init_snapshot, entry)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules — message persistence and broadcast
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A message is committed to the SQLite database. The sequence_id was
-- pre-allocated by SseBroadcaster.next_seq() before the DB write;
-- this rule models the completion of that write.
rule MessageCommittedToDb {
    when: MessageCommittedToDb(conversation_id, message_id, sequence_id)

    requires: sequence_id > 0

    ensures: PersistedMessage.created(
        conversation_id: conversation_id,
        message_id: message_id,
        sequence_id: sequence_id
    )
}

-- After a message is persisted, a message event is broadcast to every
-- open, initialized stream for this conversation. A StreamMessage join
-- entity is created for each delivery, anchoring PersistBeforeBroadcast.
--
-- Persisted message broadcast also anchors the conversation's
-- ReplayRing: a Message with a backing PersistedMessage is durable
-- (the next reconnect's init reads it from the DB), so any prior
-- ephemeral events between the previous anchor and this message are
-- no longer needed for replay. See PersistedMessageClearsReplayRing.
rule MessageBroadcast {
    when: msg: PersistedMessage.created

    ensures:
        for stream in SseStreams where conversation_id = msg.conversation_id and status = open and init_sent:
            StreamMessage.created(
                stream: stream,
                message_id: msg.message_id,
                sequence_id: msg.sequence_id
            )
            stream.last_delivered_seq = msg.sequence_id

    @guidance
        -- Implementation: SseBroadcaster.send_persisted_message(message)
        -- is called after db.save_message() returns Ok (src/runtime.rs).
        -- The chain PersistedMessage.created → MessageBroadcast mirrors
        -- this: the DB commit is the enabling condition; the broadcast
        -- follows.
        --
        -- The message event's envelope sequence_id equals the message's
        -- own sequence_id: SseWireEvent::Message { sequence_id, message }
        -- where sequence_id = message.sequence_id (see src/api/wire.rs).
        --
        -- send_persisted_message is the dual of send_ephemeral_message
        -- (Q1 in open questions): the former resets the ReplayRing
        -- anchor; the latter appends to the ring without clearing it.
        -- Both emit SseWireEvent::Message; the distinction lives only
        -- in the broadcaster's ring lifecycle. The client cannot
        -- distinguish them on the wire (SseMessageDedupReplay handles
        -- the case where the same message_id is later re-broadcast as
        -- a persisted Message after its checkpoint completes).
}

-- A message's mutable fields (display_data, content) changed.
-- A message_updated event is broadcast to open streams.
rule MessageUpdatedBroadcast {
    when: MessageFieldsMutated(conv_id, message_id, seq)

    requires: seq > 0

    ensures:
        for stream in SseStreams where conversation_id = conv_id and status = open and init_sent:
            stream.last_delivered_seq = seq

    @guidance
        -- message_updated is only meaningful after the corresponding
        -- message event has been delivered (or is present in the init
        -- snapshot). A client that receives message_updated before
        -- message treats it as a silent no-op
        -- (SseMessageUpdatedUnknownTarget in conversation_atom.allium).
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules — ReplayRing lifecycle
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Every persisted Message broadcast resets the conversation's
-- ReplayRing: the anchor advances to the new message's seq, the
-- entries list clears, the truncated flag clears.
--
-- The narrow guarantee this captures: every event the client could
-- ever need to replay sits in (anchor, current_tip]. Anything at or
-- below anchor is durable in the DB and reads back via init's
-- messages.
rule PersistedMessageClearsReplayRing {
    when: msg: PersistedMessage.created

    let ring = ReplayRing{conversation_id: msg.conversation_id}

    ensures: ring.anchor_sequence_id = msg.sequence_id
    ensures: ring.entries = empty_list
    ensures: ring.truncated = false

    @guidance
        -- Implementation: SseBroadcaster.send_persisted_message clears
        -- the ring before publishing. Order matters: clearing first
        -- ensures that any concurrent subscribe seeing the ring will
        -- either see the prior state (entries above the old anchor) or
        -- the cleared state (entries above the new anchor) — never a
        -- spurious mix in which the new Message's seq is also in
        -- `entries` (which would double-deliver it through the
        -- client's pending replay path).
}

-- Every non-Message broadcast appends to the ring. Includes:
-- token, state_change, message_updated, agent_done, conversation_update,
-- conversation_became_terminal, error, browser_session_state,
-- steer_message_queued. Excludes Init (per-stream, never broadcast)
-- and ConversationHardDeleted (terminal action; no subsequent init
-- can fire for this conversation, so replay is moot).
--
-- Append happens atomically with broadcast: the entry's seq IS the
-- envelope seq the live subscribers receive.
rule EphemeralEventAppendedToReplayRing {
    when: EphemeralEventBroadcast(conv_id, entry)

    requires: entry.event_type in {
        "token", "state_change", "message_updated", "agent_done",
        "conversation_update", "conversation_became_terminal", "error",
        "browser_session_state", "steer_message_queued"
    }

    let ring = ReplayRing{conversation_id: conv_id}

    requires: entry.sequence_id > ring.anchor_sequence_id

    -- Append until the cap; on overflow, drop the ring entirely and
    -- enter "truncated" mode. The next subscribe will see
    -- pending_events = empty list and pending_truncated = true,
    -- which forces a full resync on the client side (Q3 resolution).
    ensures: ring.entries = append_or_clear(ring.entries, entry,
                                            config.replay_ring_capacity,
                                            ring.truncated)
    ensures: ring.truncated = ring.truncated or
                length(ring.entries) >= config.replay_ring_capacity

    @guidance
        -- append_or_clear(list, entry, cap, already_truncated):
        --   if already_truncated:               return list  (no-op)
        --   if length(list) + 1 < cap:          return list ++ [entry]
        --   if length(list) + 1 == cap:         return list ++ [entry]
        --                                       (this entry fills the
        --                                       ring; subsequent
        --                                       appends will trip the
        --                                       overflow branch)
        --   if length(list) + 1 > cap:          return empty_list
        --                                       (truncate-and-clear;
        --                                       ring is now dead until
        --                                       PersistedMessageClearsReplayRing
        --                                       resets it)
        --
        -- Note that truncate-and-clear is irreversible within a
        -- single anchor window: once ring.truncated = true, no more
        -- appends accumulate. The flag clears only via
        -- PersistedMessageClearsReplayRing.
        --
        -- Implementation should also emit a tracing::warn! on the
        -- truncation transition (one-shot per anchor window) so ops
        -- can detect pathological cases.
        --
        -- Per Q2 resolution: track aggregate serialised bytes of
        -- ring entries as a metric (debug-level tracing counter on
        -- every append, gauge for current ring byte size). Bytes
        -- are observability-only — the cap is enforced by entry
        -- count, not bytes.
}

-- Eager (non-persisted) Message broadcast appends to the ring without
-- resetting the anchor. The accompanying PersistedMessage with the
-- same message_id (emitted later via the persist_checkpoint path)
-- will fire PersistedMessageClearsReplayRing and discard this entry.
--
-- The narrow purpose: the runtime's Effect::BroadcastAssistantMessage
-- needs the in-flight assistant content (text + tool_use blocks) to
-- reach reconnecting clients during a tool round — not just live
-- subscribers. Without ring residency, the bridge between
-- "LLM responded" and "tool round checkpoint" is invisible to a client
-- that disconnects in that window.
rule EagerAssistantMessageAppendedToReplayRing {
    when: EagerAssistantMessageBroadcast(conv_id, entry)

    requires: entry.event_type = "message"
    requires: not exists PersistedMessage{message_id: entry.payload.message_id, conversation_id: conv_id}

    let ring = ReplayRing{conversation_id: conv_id}

    requires: entry.sequence_id > ring.anchor_sequence_id

    -- Same overflow semantics as EphemeralEventAppendedToReplayRing:
    -- if the append would exceed the cap, clear the ring and set
    -- truncated; if already truncated, the append is a no-op.
    ensures: ring.entries = append_or_clear(ring.entries, entry,
                                            config.replay_ring_capacity,
                                            ring.truncated)
    ensures: ring.truncated = ring.truncated or
                length(ring.entries) >= config.replay_ring_capacity

    @guidance
        -- Implementation: SseBroadcaster.send_ephemeral_message,
        -- called from Effect::BroadcastAssistantMessage in
        -- src/runtime/executor.rs after the LLM response lands but
        -- before tool execution begins. The DB write is deferred to
        -- Effect::PersistCheckpoint (atomic with the round's
        -- tool_results), which goes through send_persisted_message.
        --
        -- The client's SseMessageDedupReplay rule
        -- (conversation_atom.allium) is what handles the eventual
        -- duplicate: the eager Message and the persisted Message
        -- carry the same message_id, so the second arrival is a no-op
        -- on `messages` (still advances lastSequenceId).

    -- Open Q1 in the questions section: an alternative shape would
    -- give the wire two variants (message_ephemeral, message). Today
    -- we share SseWireEvent::Message and distinguish only at the
    -- broadcaster API. If a future feature needs a client-visible
    -- distinction (e.g. "do not show this as a permanent message in
    -- the share view"), promote to two variants.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules — state machine events
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A conversation phase transition is broadcast as state_change.
rule StateChangeBroadcast {
    when: ConversationStateTransitioned(conv_id, new_state, seq)

    requires: seq > 0

    ensures:
        for stream in SseStreams where conversation_id = conv_id and status = open and init_sent:
            stream.last_delivered_seq = seq

    @guidance
        -- new_state is the opaque ConvState discriminated union. The
        -- client routes it through parseConversationState for validation.
        -- display_state is a human-readable string derived from new_state.
}

-- When a conversation reaches a terminal state, the terminal event is
-- broadcast. The terminal subsystem uses this to tear down PTYs.
rule ConversationBecameTerminalBroadcast {
    when: ConversationEnteredTerminalState(conv_id, seq)

    requires: seq > 0

    ensures:
        for stream in SseStreams where conversation_id = conv_id and status = open and init_sent:
            stream.last_delivered_seq = seq
}

-- Partial conversation metadata changes are broadcast as conversation_update.
-- The client merges the payload shallowly onto its local Conversation copy.
rule ConversationUpdateBroadcast {
    when: ConversationMetadataChanged(conv_id, update, seq)

    requires: seq > 0

    ensures:
        for stream in SseStreams where conversation_id = conv_id and status = open and init_sent:
            stream.last_delivered_seq = seq
            stream.last_update = update
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules — LLM turn events
------------------------------------------------------------

-- During an LLM turn, each streaming token is broadcast as a token
-- event. Tokens are ephemeral: not persisted to SQLite and not
-- included in reconnect init snapshots.
rule TokenBroadcast {
    when: LlmTokenReceived(conv_id, request_id, text, seq)

    requires: seq > 0

    ensures:
        for stream in SseStreams where conversation_id = conv_id and status = open and init_sent:
            stream.last_delivered_seq = seq

    @guidance
        -- Tokens share the monotonic sequence counter with persisted
        -- events (SseBroadcaster.send_seq). A reconnecting client's
        -- applyIfNewer guard silently drops tokens whose seq <=
        -- lastSequenceId — no gap visible to the user.
        --
        -- No StreamMessage is created for tokens: they are ephemeral
        -- and PersistBeforeBroadcast does not apply to them.
}

-- When an LLM turn completes, agent_done is broadcast. The client uses
-- this to clear the streaming buffer and transition phase to idle.
rule AgentDoneBroadcast {
    when: LlmTurnCompleted(conv_id, seq)

    requires: seq > 0

    ensures:
        for stream in SseStreams where conversation_id = conv_id and status = open and init_sent:
            stream.last_delivered_seq = seq

    @guidance
        -- agent_done is emitted after the final message has been
        -- persisted and broadcast (MessageBroadcast fires first via
        -- the PersistedMessage.created chain). The client's
        -- SseAgentDoneApplied rule clears streamingBuffer regardless
        -- of whether sse_message arrived (replay recovery).
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules — error events
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A user-facing error is broadcast as an error event.
rule ErrorBroadcast {
    when: UserFacingErrorOccurred(conv_id, error, seq)

    requires: seq > 0

    ensures:
        for stream in SseStreams where conversation_id = conv_id and status = open and init_sent:
            stream.last_delivered_seq = seq
            stream.last_error = error

    @guidance
        -- The error payload carries both a flat message string (for the
        -- existing toast) and the typed UserFacingError (for future
        -- kind-aware affordances). On reconnect, the error's sequence_id
        -- routes through applyIfNewer — a replayed error is dropped.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- LOAD-BEARING: every message-type SSE event delivered on a stream
-- (recorded as a StreamMessage) has a corresponding PersistedMessage
-- in the database. This formalises the persist-before-broadcast
-- ordering: the DB write commits before the broadcast fires.
--
-- Violation scenario (task 02679 hypothesis): MessageBroadcast fires
-- before db.save_message() completes. A client that reconnects during
-- the broadcast-lag close window issues a fresh init. The init reads
-- from a DB that has not yet committed the message — the message
-- appears to vanish from the conversation view.
--
-- The structural model captures this: a StreamMessage exists iff
-- MessageBroadcast ran; a PersistedMessage exists iff
-- MessageCommittedToDb ran. This invariant requires both to exist
-- simultaneously, enforcing the ordering.
invariant PersistBeforeBroadcast {
    for stream in SseStreams:
        for sm in stream.stream_messages:
            exists PersistedMessage{message_id: sm.message_id, conversation_id: stream.conversation_id}
}

-- The init event is always the first event delivered on a stream.
-- Structurally enforced: StreamOpened sets init_sent = true at
-- stream creation, simultaneously with last_delivered_seq. A stream
-- that has delivered any event (last_delivered_seq > 0) necessarily
-- has init_sent = true.
invariant InitAlwaysFirst {
    for stream in SseStreams:
        not stream.init_sent implies stream.last_delivered_seq = 0
}

-- Sequence IDs are non-negative on all streams.
invariant SequencesNonNegative {
    for stream in SseStreams:
        stream.last_delivered_seq >= 0
}

-- ReplayRing size is bounded by the configured capacity. Append paths
-- evict oldest-first when length would exceed the cap, so this invariant
-- is enforced structurally by append_bounded (see
-- EphemeralEventAppendedToReplayRing / EagerAssistantMessageAppendedToReplayRing).
invariant ReplayRingBounded {
    for ring in ReplayRings:
        length(ring.entries) <= config.replay_ring_capacity
}

-- Every entry in a ring has a sequence_id strictly greater than the
-- ring's anchor. PersistedMessageClearsReplayRing resets the anchor
-- and empties entries simultaneously; append paths require the new
-- entry's seq > anchor by precondition. A violation would mean an
-- entry survived a checkpoint that should have evicted it — a replay
-- of pending events on init could then re-deliver a Message the
-- client already has in `messages` from the DB read.
invariant ReplayRingEntriesAboveAnchor {
    for ring in ReplayRings:
        for entry in ring.entries:
            entry.sequence_id > ring.anchor_sequence_id
}

-- The init snapshot's pending_anchor matches the conversation's ring
-- anchor at the moment the snapshot was taken. Pending entries are
-- mirrored as well, but list-content equivalence is not expressible
-- in this dialect — it is guarded structurally by the snapshot
-- constructor reading ring.entries directly (see StreamOpened's
-- `requires:` clauses for the per-entry sequence_id bounds).
invariant InitSnapshotMirrorsRing {
    for stream in SseStreams:
        for ring in ReplayRings:
            ring.conversation_id = stream.conversation_id
                implies stream.init_snapshot.pending_anchor_sequence_id = ring.anchor_sequence_id
}

-- Ring entries are in strictly increasing sequence_id order. Not
-- expressible as a structural invariant in this dialect (no list
-- indexing primitive). Guarded by construction:
--   - SseBroadcaster.next_seq allocates via fetch_add(1), so
--     successive entries have strictly increasing seqs.
--   - append_bounded appends at the tail; eviction (oldest-first)
--     drops only the head.
--   - PersistedMessageClearsReplayRing resets `entries = empty_list`
--     atomically with the anchor advance, so no out-of-order entry
--     can survive an anchor reset.
-- A future change that violates any of those three would need a
-- corresponding behavioural test (see test_replay_ring_order in
-- src/runtime.rs once the implementation lands).

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------
-- Kept as prose rather than `open question` declarations so the file
-- remains parseable. These are design-intent clarifications discovered
-- during the ReplayRing extension; each either needs explicit
-- confirmation before the implementation lands or is a deferred
-- follow-up the next reader should be aware of.
--
-- Q1. Wire shape: shared `message` event vs split
--     `message_ephemeral` / `message`. Today the eager assistant
--     broadcast and the persisted Message both emit
--     SseWireEvent::Message. The distinction lives only in the
--     broadcaster's ring lifecycle (send_ephemeral_message appends,
--     send_persisted_message anchors + appends through MessageBroadcast).
--     Pro shared: smaller wire surface; client cannot accidentally
--       branch on "is this real?" — the persisted vs ephemeral
--       distinction is a server-side hygiene concern.
--     Pro split: lets future UI features render eager Messages
--       distinctly (e.g., a faint "in-flight" treatment, or omitting
--       them from a Share view). Today no consumer needs this.
--     Decision: stay with shared. Promote to split only if a
--     concrete consumer needs it.
--
-- Q2. ReplayRing capacity unit: event count vs bytes. Today: count
--     (512 entries). Tokens dominate the entry count during LLM
--     streaming; each is small (~tens of bytes serialised). A pure
--     count cap exposes us to pathological cases where a tool result
--     with a large payload counts the same as one token. If that
--     ever becomes a real failure mode, switch to a hybrid
--     (max_entries OR max_bytes, whichever hits first). Today: count.
--
--     RESOLVED 2026-05-10: cap by count; ALSO track aggregate
--     serialised bytes as an observability metric so we can detect
--     the pathological case (large-event-dominated rings) before it
--     causes a memory issue. Surface via the existing tracing
--     subscriber as a debug counter on every append, and as a gauge
--     readable by an ops-side dashboard if one is later added. No
--     enforcement on bytes today; metric-only.
--
-- Q3. RESOLVED 2026-05-10: on ring overflow, force a full resync —
--     do not deliver partial pending_events. Once the ring exceeds
--     `replay_ring_capacity` between two persisted Message anchors,
--     the ring transitions to a "truncated" state where:
--       (a) further appends are no-ops (ring entries do not grow),
--       (b) ring.entries is cleared,
--       (c) ring.truncated is set true.
--     The init snapshot then carries pending_events = empty list and
--     pending_truncated = true. The client renders the DB snapshot
--     and waits for the next persisted Message (or state_change /
--     other live events) to repopulate the UI.
--
--     Rationale: partial replay was deemed misleading — the user
--     would see an incomplete in-progress view that "fills in" later
--     in a way that's hard to distinguish from a bug. Full resync is
--     a clean, consistent fallback: the only thing the user
--     temporarily loses is the in-flight streaming preview, and the
--     next Message broadcast restores authoritative state.
--
-- Q4. Per-event vs per-byte cap. See Q2. Bytes would also handle
--     pathological event sizes; punted to a follow-up.
--
-- Q5. RESOLVED 2026-05-10: accept as-is. The ring is in-process
--     memory only; a server restart loses it. On restart the next
--     subscribe gets an empty pending_events and the client falls
--     back to DB-only reconstruction. This is correct by
--     construction: the underlying LLM stream itself did not survive
--     the restart (the in-flight call is lost), so a recovered ring
--     would replay events for a turn that is restarting from
--     scratch anyway. The state machine's existing recovery path
--     (ConvState persisted, retry LlmRequesting) handles the rest.
--     A future "graceful restart" feature that preserves in-flight
--     LLM turns across restarts would need to revisit this — flagged
--     for that hypothetical work.
